    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#herodotus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodotus.jpg" alt="Herodotus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#herodotus">Herodotus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_bust_of_Herodotos_MET_DT11742_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Chios is a large, mountainous island in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aegean">Aegean</a>, separated from the Erythrae peninsula of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> by a strait only a few kilometres wide. It was inhabited from the Neolithic, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> settlement at Emporio on its south coast had contacts with the Minoan and Mycenaean worlds. Greek tradition, reported by the fifth-century Chian writer Ion of Chios, made Oenopion, a son of Dionysus who came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crete">Crete</a>, the island's first king and the man who taught its people to make wine, and spoke of later Carian and Abantian settlers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ionia">Ionian Greeks</a> settled the island around 1000 BCE, and Chios became one of the twelve members of the Ionian League, which met at the Panionion on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mount-mycale">Mount Mycale</a>. The chief city grew up on the east coast facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a>, on the site of the modern town of Chios, while smaller communities, such as the fortified hilltop village excavated at Emporio, occupied the rest of the island. By the sixth century BCE Chios was one of the richest states in the Greek world, and it held land on the mainland opposite: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#herodotus">Herodotus</a> records that it gained the district of Atarneus from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persians">Persians</a> in return for handing over the Lydian rebel Pactyes, an act later remembered as shameful.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#black-sea"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-sea.jpg" alt="Black Sea" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#black-sea">Black Sea</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Danube_Spills_into_the_Black_Sea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The SeaWiFS Project, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The wealth of Chios rested on agriculture for export. Chian wine was among the most prized in the Greek world, shipped in distinctive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#amphora">amphorae</a> that archaeologists find from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#black-sea">Black Sea</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>, and the island was the only source of mastic, an aromatic resin from trees in its southern villages that was used in medicine and as a chewing gum. Chian merchants traded widely: Chios was one of the cities that shared the Hellenion sanctuary at Naucratis in Egypt, and its pottery and coinage, which carried a sphinx beside a wine amphora, travelled across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aegean">Aegean</a>. The historian Theopompus, himself a Chian, claimed that the Chians were the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#greeks">Greeks</a> after the Spartans and Thessalians to use slaves, and the first to buy them from abroad rather than subjugate a native population, and Thucydides wrote that it had more slaves than any Greek state except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sparta">Sparta</a>. Chios was also a centre of craft and culture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#herodotus">Herodotus</a> credits Glaucus of Chios with inventing the welding of iron, a family of sculptors descended from Archermus worked there in the sixth century BCE, and the Homeridae, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guild">guild</a> of rhapsodes who claimed descent from Homer, kept the island's claim to be his birthplace. Later Chians included the tragedian Ion, the mathematician Hippocrates of Chios and the historian Theopompus.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-lade"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lade.jpg" alt="Battle of Lade" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-lade">Battle of Lade</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ionian_revolt_Battle_of_Lade.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ionian_Revolt_Campaign_Map-fr.svg: Eric Gaba (Sting - fr:Sting) derivative work: MinisterForBadTimes (talk), CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Chios was one of the leading naval powers of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aegean">Aegean</a>. Its fleet was its main instrument of policy, and its citizens were experienced seamen. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lydia">Lydia</a> in 546 BCE, Chios, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#herodotus">Herodotus</a>, submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> of its own accord, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persians">Persians</a> as yet had no fleet. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a> it made the largest contribution of any state to the rebel fleet, sending 100 ships to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-lade">Battle of Lade</a> in 494 BCE, where, Herodotus says, its crews fought bravely after others deserted; the Persians ravaged the island the following year. After the Battle of Mycale in 479 BCE Chios joined the Greek alliance and then the Delian League, providing ships rather than tribute. It helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#athens">Athens</a> suppress the revolt of Samos in 440 BCE and sent squadrons to Athenian expeditions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>. In 412 BCE, after the Athenian disaster in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sicily">Sicily</a>, Chios revolted with Spartan support; the Athenians landed, defeated the Chians in several battles and ravaged the countryside, and the island served as a Spartan base until 404 BCE. In the Social War its fleet beat off an Athenian attack on the harbour of Chios in 357 BCE, in which the Athenian general Chabrias was killed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#darius-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darius-i.jpg" alt="Darius I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#darius-i">Darius I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darius_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rumlu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Chios was ruled by kings in its early history and later by an aristocracy, but it developed popular institutions early. An inscription of about 575–550 BCE refers to a popular council (<em>boule demosie</em>) that met monthly, heard appeals and handled public business, alongside magistrates called <em>demarchoi</em> and <em>basileis</em>; it is one of the earliest pieces of evidence for a council of this kind outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#athens">Athens</a>. Under Persian rule the island was governed by a tyrant, Strattis, who joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#darius-i">Darius I</a> on his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dariuss-scythian-campaign">Scythian campaign</a> around 513 BCE and later faced a plot to overthrow him. In the fifth century Chios was known for stable, moderate government, probably a broad oligarchy, and Thucydides praised the Chians as the only people besides the Spartans who managed prosperity with prudence. Within the Delian League Chios kept its own fleet and full autonomy, and after Athens disarmed Samos and Lesbos it remained the last important ally with that status. In the fourth century democrats and oligarchs alternated, often backed by Athens, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> or Caria. Chios was a founding member of the Second Athenian League in 378 BCE. A letter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, preserved in an inscription and probably written between 334 and 332 BCE, orders the return of exiles and the drafting of a democratic constitution.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#rome"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rome.jpg" alt="Rome" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trevi_Fountain,_Rome,_Italy_2_-_May_2007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diliff, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Chios mattered to the Greek world above all through its wealth, its fleet and its culture. Its wine set a standard that later writers, from comic poets to Roman authors, mentioned as a byword for luxury, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#amphora">amphorae</a> are among the most useful markers archaeologists have for dating and tracing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aegean">Aegean</a> trade. Its claim to Homer, supported by the line in the Homeric Hymn to Apollo about the blind poet who lives on rocky Chios and by the Homeridae, made the island a centre for the recitation of epic poetry and shaped ancient ideas of where the Homeric poems came from. The inscription mentioning a popular council is a key document for historians of early Greek constitutional development. Chian writers and thinkers, among them Ion, Hippocrates, the astronomer Oenopides and the philosopher Metrodorus, contributed to Greek poetry, mathematics and science. Chios' heavy dependence on bought slaves made it an important case in ancient and modern discussions of chattel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#slavery">slavery</a>, and later sources describe slave revolts on the island. After the end of its independence the island remained prosperous under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, and its mastic trade later drew the Genoese, who ruled it from 1346 to 1566, before the Ottoman conquest.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#peloponnesian-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war.jpg" alt="Peloponnesian War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pelop_war_en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Translator was Kenmayer, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Chios lost real independence gradually as larger powers came to dominate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aegean">Aegean</a>. Its revolt from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#athens">Athens</a> in 412 BCE brought invasion and devastation, and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> left it under Spartan control for a time. In the fourth century it regained its fleet and freedom, leaving the Second Athenian League in the Social War of 357–355 BCE with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mausolus">Mausolus</a> of Caria, but soon fell under the influence of Carian dynasts and Persian-backed oligarchs. In 333 BCE Persian commanders seized the island, and it was retaken for Alexander in 332 BCE. Under the Hellenistic kings Chios remained a nominally free city, carefully balancing between rival dynasties and later cultivating friendship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a>, which it supported against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>. The final blow came in the First Mithridatic War. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a>, who suspected the Chians of favouring Rome, imposed a heavy fine in 86 BCE and then had his general Zenobius deport the population towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#black-sea">Black Sea</a>; according to Memnon of Heraclea, many were freed on the way by the people of Heraclea Pontica. After the war Sulla restored the Chians to their island, and Rome recognised Chios as a free city, but it was now one of many communities under Roman supremacy.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Chios was one of the wealthiest and most stable Greek island states, a major naval power at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-lade">Battle of Lade</a> and in the Delian League, famous for its wine, mastic and the Homeridae, and an early example of both popular institutions and large-scale chattel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#slavery">slavery</a>.</em></p>

